WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.230
Musa says it rained a lot last night.

2
00:00:03.930 --> 00:00:05.870
Zainab says only a little.

3
00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.260
Both families farm, so it matters.

4
00:00:10.010 --> 00:00:10.510
Who’s right?

5
00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:12.870
Today we stop guessing.

6
00:00:14.150 --> 00:00:16.930
Today: build a rain gauge from a plastic

7
00:00:16.930 --> 00:00:19.770
bottle — and measure rain in millimetres.

8
00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:23.350
Rainfall is measured in millimetres.

9
00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:26.220
Ten millimetres of rain means that, on

10
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:28.840
flat ground, the water would stand ten

11
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.360
millimetres deep — if none ran off,

12
00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:33.000
soaked in or dried up.

13
00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.690
So we measure depth, not amount.

14
00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:39.010
A wide bottle catches more

15
00:00:39.010 --> 00:00:40.470
water than a narrow one.

16
00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:43.650
But if both have straight sides, the water

17
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.550
stands at the same depth in each.

18
00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:49.230
A rain gauge is a container that collects

19
00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:51.370
rain so you can measure its depth.

20
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.460
And you can make one from a

21
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:54.660
clear plastic bottle.

22
00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:57.910
Choose a bottle with straight sides.

23
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.880
An adult cuts off the top where it starts

24
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:03.300
to curve, and tapes the sharp edge.

25
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.000
Add a few stones, then water just to

26
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:09.340
cover them — mark that level zero.

27
00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:12.740
Tape a millimetre scale up from zero.

28
00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:15.850
Turn the top upside down to make a funnel.

29
00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:17.430
Quick check!

30
00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.230
I’ve got two bottles: one with

31
00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.110
straight sides, one with a

32
00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:23.390
narrow waist in the middle.

33
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.420
Which makes the better rain gauge?

34
00:01:26.680 --> 00:01:27.900
The straight-sided one.

35
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.980
Where a bottle narrows, a little

36
00:01:30.980 --> 00:01:33.520
rain rises a long way up the scale,

37
00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:35.920
so the reading is too high.

38
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.540
Straight sides keep every millimetre true.

39
00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:42.400
Now, where to put it.

40
00:01:43.030 --> 00:01:45.590
Choose open ground, well away

41
00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:47.930
from roofs, walls and trees.

42
00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:49.780
Trees block the rain.

43
00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:52.540
Roofs drip extra water in.

44
00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:55.660
Stand it upright and firm, with

45
00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.080
stones around it if it’s windy.

46
00:01:58.360 --> 00:01:59.340
Then the routine.

47
00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.710
Read it at the same time every day

48
00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.970
— say, 8 am — at eye level.

49
00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:08.470
Write the millimetres and the date.

50
00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:12.020
Then pour it back down to zero, so

51
00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:13.760
no rain is counted twice.

52
00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.740
Once a week, empty it fully and refill to

53
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.120
zero, so mosquitoes can’t breed.

54
00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:21.950
Your turn.

55
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:25.340
My pupils want to put the gauge under the

56
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.580
big neem tree, where it’s safe from goats.

57
00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:29.020
Good idea?

58
00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.210
Not a good idea — the

59
00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:33.410
leaves catch the rain.

60
00:02:34.040 --> 00:02:36.540
Choose an open spot, and keep

61
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.040
goats away another way.

62
00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:41.170
Remember: straight sides, a

63
00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:44.230
funnel, a millimetre scale, open

64
00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:46.750
ground, same time every day.

65
00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.160
Try it in class tomorrow:

66
00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:50.900
build one gauge together.

67
00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:54.140
Choose a spot as a class, and give each

68
00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.060
pupil a day on the reading rota.

69
00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:58.100
Next lesson, we’ll turn your

70
00:02:58.100 --> 00:02:59.280
readings into a chart.
